# Add Language Feature

## Purpose

Use this skill when implementing a new VS Code language feature provider for the xcaffold extension. Language feature providers respond to editor events (cursor hover, keystroke, save) and surface information or edits back to the editor.

## Prerequisites

- xcaffold-vscode repo checked out and `npm install` completed
- `npm run typecheck` passing on the current branch
- `npm run test` passing on the current branch
- VS Code API reference consulted (`xcaf/skills/add-language-feature/references/vscode-api-tiers.md`)

## Steps

### 1. Identify the VS Code language API

Decide which provider interface to implement. Consult `xcaf/skills/add-language-feature/references/vscode-api-tiers.md` for the full list and priority tiers. Confirm the interface name (e.g., `vscode.CompletionItemProvider`, `vscode.HoverProvider`) before writing any code.

### 2. Create the provider file

Create `src/<name>Provider.ts`. Name the file using the provider's short function name in camelCase, for example `completionProvider.ts` or `hoverProvider.ts`.

Extract all parsing or computation logic into a **pure function** at the top of the file. The pure function takes the document text and position as plain arguments (no VS Code API types) so it can be unit-tested without a VS Code host.

```ts
// Pure function — testable without VS Code
export function resolveCompletionItems(lineText: string, position: number): string[] {
    // ... logic here
}

// Provider class — thin wrapper
export class XcafCompletionProvider implements vscode.CompletionItemProvider {
    provideCompletionItems(
        document: vscode.TextDocument,
        position: vscode.Position
    ): vscode.CompletionItem[] {
        const line = document.lineAt(position).text;
        const names = resolveCompletionItems(line, position.character);
        return names.map(n => new vscode.CompletionItem(n, vscode.CompletionItemKind.Field));
    }
}
```

### 3. Follow the pure function extraction pattern

Parsing logic MUST live in a pure function separate from the provider class. This is non-negotiable: the test suite runs under ts-node without a VS Code host, so provider classes that call VS Code APIs in their constructor or static methods cannot be instantiated in tests.

### 4. Register the provider in extension.ts

Add the registration call inside `activate()`. Use `vscode.languages.register<Type>Provider` with a document selector scoped to `.xcaf` files:

```ts
const completionProvider = vscode.languages.registerCompletionItemProvider(
    { scheme: 'file', pattern: '**/*.xcaf' },
    new XcafCompletionProvider(index),
    '.', ':'   // trigger characters, if any
);
context.subscriptions.push(completionProvider);
```

The disposable returned by `registerXxxProvider` MUST be pushed to `context.subscriptions`. Never hold it in a module-level variable.

### 5. Add package.json contributes if needed

Some providers require a `package.json` entry in the `contributes` section. Check:

| Provider Type | Needs package.json entry? |
|---|---|
| CompletionItemProvider | No |
| HoverProvider | No |
| DocumentSymbolProvider | No |
| ReferenceProvider | No |
| RenameProvider | No |
| CodeActionProvider | Yes — add `codeActionsProvider` entry |
| SemanticTokensProvider | Yes — declare `semanticTokenTypes` and `semanticTokenModifiers` |
| FileDecorationProvider | No |
| DocumentFormattingEditProvider | No |

### 6. Create the test file

Create `src/test/<name>Provider.test.ts`. Import and test the pure function directly. Do not instantiate the provider class in tests.

```ts
import * as assert from 'assert';
import { resolveCompletionItems } from '../../<name>Provider';

suite('<Name>Provider', () => {
    test('returns field names for empty line', () => {
        const result = resolveCompletionItems('', 0);
        assert.ok(result.length > 0);
    });

    test('returns empty array for unknown context', () => {
        const result = resolveCompletionItems('random text with no xcaf context', 5);
        assert.deepStrictEqual(result, []);
    });
});
```

### 7. Run verification

Both commands must exit 0 before the work is considered complete:

```bash
npm run typecheck   # tsc --noEmit
npm run test        # Mocha suite
```

If typecheck fails, fix all type errors before running tests. TypeScript strict mode is enforced — no `any` escapes.

## Anti-Patterns

| Anti-Pattern | Why It Fails |
|---|---|
| Putting parsing logic inside the provider class | Cannot unit-test without a VS Code host; forces integration tests |
| Using `any` to bypass strict TypeScript | Violates the zero-`any` rule; typecheck will fail |
| Importing from `src/test/` in production code | Test utilities are not for production use |
| Adding a runtime `dependencies` entry in package.json | Extension ships with zero runtime deps; use esbuild to bundle devDependencies |
| Registering the provider outside of `activate()` | Side effects at import time violate the no-side-effects-at-import rule |
| Using VS Code APIs introduced after 1.85.0 without bumping engines.vscode | Breaks the declared minimum engine version |
